  {
    "episode_id": 86305,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/6/86305.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 19,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think more and more Americans are deeply frustrated by what they see online and what they see as a monopoly out of California, controlling more and more of the information that comes to us, allowing us to see certain information and borrowing other information, promoting certain voices as we found out and not promoting other voices. And I think what I love about our local community broadcasters, what I love about the radio station right here, is if somebody doesn't like something, they can come down here and talk to you about it, right? They can reach out via email. They can reach out via online. I want to make sure that we have these local businesses so that people are responsive to the needs of Americans and Hoosiers. Facebook has proven they aren't. They don't care. And I think as we've seen in recent Senate testimony, in fact, they're going the exact opposite direction. They found out that Instagram is really, really addictive. They tried to make it even more addictive. They found out that pre-teens really, really like Instagram. They tried to push more products on those pre-teens. They found out that violence and really rancorous language is what gets promoted more and more on Facebook. So they found a way to drive even more of that. These are things that you get from a company that's 2,000 miles away in California that you wouldn't get if that was a local small business right here in our communities.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
